# PAY — HTTP 402 and x402 v2

File: guide/Pay.oot
User wiki. Canonical URL: https://openooda.org/guide/#Pay.oot

[Home](Home.oot) [Start](Use.oot) [Language](Language.oot) [Commands](Options.oot) [Packages](Packages.oot) [Capabilities](Safety.oot) [How it works](Limits.oot)

This page is how an `.oo` program reads an HTTP 402 and names a price.
It also covers capability quotes, hashcash work, inverted offers, 425 Too Early, and Privacy Pass scrape.
It is not a wallet. It does not send money by itself.
The helpers live in `ooda/std` (`std/net/x402.oo`, `std/net/x402_spend.oo`).
They speak **x402 v2**.

Spec: https://github.com/x402-foundation/x402/blob/main/specs/x402-specification-v2.md
HTTP transport: https://github.com/x402-foundation/x402/blob/main/specs/transports-v2/http.md

## 1. What 402 is

HTTP 402 means payment required.
x402 v2 is a handshake on that status: the server quotes a price, the client may attach a signed payload and retry.

openOODA teaches the program to **see** the quote and to **refuse spend** unless you pin it.
It does not put a wallet on disk when you install.
It does not charge the public door.
A tip quote Worker is `GET https://pay.openooda.org/tip`.
After you set the `PAY_TO` secret it returns HTTP 402 plus a `PAYMENT-REQUIRED` header. Until then it is 503.
That is not the door. Authors put their own `payTo` on a package manifest.

v2 headers:

| Header | Direction | Contents |
|---|---|---|
| `PAYMENT-REQUIRED` | server → client | base64 `PaymentRequired` JSON |
| `PAYMENT-SIGNATURE` | client → server | base64 `PaymentPayload` JSON |
| `PAYMENT-RESPONSE` | server → client | base64 settlement JSON |

This library does not send `PAYMENT-RESPONSE`. That header is a settlement receipt. We do not settle.

## 2. Import

```
import "std/net/x402.oo";
import "std/net/x402_spend.oo";
```

These files are on `main`.
They are not in the v0.204.0-beta tag until the next pin.

## 3. See a 402

`x402_is_payment_required(status)` is true only for `402`.
`http_response_reason(402)` is `Payment Required`.

Decode the `PAYMENT-REQUIRED` header to JSON, then parse:

```
let q: X402Quote = x402_parse_quote(body);
if x402_is_payment_required(402) {
    if x402_quote_ready(q) {
        println(x402_format_quote(q));
    }
}
```

`x402_quote_ready` is true when `payTo`, `amount`, `network`, `asset`, and `resource.url` are present.
`amount` is atomic token units. `network` is CAIP-2 (`eip155:8453` is Base).
`x402_format_quote` prints one line: amount, network, pay-to address.

A fixture you can parse without the network: `fixtures/x402_quote.json`.
Check: `ooda run scripts/x402_parse_smoke.oo`.

## 4. Build a v2 quote (if you sell)

If your program is the seller, build the JSON, then put it in `PAYMENT-REQUIRED` (base64) on HTTP 402:

```
let body: String = x402_required_json(
    "0xYourAddress",
    "1000",
    "eip155:8453",
    "0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913",
    "https://example.com/resource"
);
```

That is a v2 `PaymentRequired` string. You still have to send HTTP 402 on your server.
GitHub Pages cannot do that handshake. A Worker or other origin can.

## 5. Spend stays off

Attaching a `PAYMENT-SIGNATURE` proof is denied unless:

1. The spend pin is exactly `1` (callers pass `OODA_X402_SPEND`).
2. The proof string is not empty.

```
let pin: String = "";
let r: Result[Bool, String] = x402_attach_or_deny(pin, "proof");
if r.is_err() {
    println("spend denied");
}
```

Empty pin → denied.
Pin `1` and empty proof → denied.
Pin `1` and a proof → `Ok(true)`. Then use `x402_payment_header_line(proof)` for `PAYMENT-SIGNATURE: …`.
That is permission to attach a header. It is not a payment.

There is no chain send and no facilitator in this library.
Install does not turn spend on.
`ooda add` prints when a package lists a price. It does not charge.

## 6. Pay with a capability

A 402 can ask for an attenuated cap instead of USDC.

```
import "std/net/pay_cap.oo";

let q: PayCapQuote = pay_cap_parse(body);
if pay_cap_ready(q) {
    println(pay_cap_format(q));
}
```

Headers: `CAP-REQUIRED`, `CAP-PROOF`.
`pay_cap_ready` needs `scheme=cap`, a cap name (`FsRead`, …), and `resource.url`.
Attach is denied unless `OODA_CAP_SPEND=1` and the proof is not empty.
This file does not check a live token.

## 7. Pay with work

A 402 can ask for hashcash (SHA-256 leading zero bits).

```
import "std/net/pay_work.oo";

let q: PayWorkQuote = pay_work_parse(body);
if pay_work_stamp_ok(stamp, q.bits, q.resource) {
    println(pay_work_format(q));
}
```

Headers: `WORK-REQUIRED`, `WORK-PROOF`.
Stamp shape: `ver:bits:date:resource:ext:rand:counter`.
Attach is denied unless `OODA_WORK_SPEND=1`.
The library verifies a stamp. It does not mint one.

## 8. Inverted 402

The server offers to pay the client. That is not an IETF status.
Use HTTP 200 plus `PAYMENT-OFFERED`.

```
import "std/net/pay_offer.oo";

let o: PayOffer = pay_offer_parse(body);
if pay_offer_ready(o) {
    println(pay_offer_format(o));
}
```

Claim is denied unless `OODA_OFFER_CLAIM=1`. Claiming is not collecting.

## 9. 425 Too Early

HTTP 425 means the proof arrived before it was valid (RFC 8470).
`http_response_reason(425)` is `Too Early`.
`pay_early_is_too_early(425)` is true. 402 is not 425.

```
import "std/net/pay_early.oo";

let e: PayEarly = pay_early_parse(body);
```

`pay_early_ready` needs `validAfter`. This file does not read a clock.

## 10. Privacy Pass

Scrape a `PrivateToken` challenge (RFC 9577 shape). Do not mint or redeem.

```
import "std/net/pay_pass.oo";

let p: PayPass = pay_pass_parse(header);
```

Ready when `challenge` and `token-key` are both present.
Attach is denied unless `OODA_PASS_ATTACH=1`.
No VOPRF and no issuer in this library.

Check all five: `ooda run scripts/pay_handshakes_smoke.oo`.

Capabilities for network fetch are on [Capabilities](Safety.oot).
First run is on [Start](Use.oot).
